        {
          "id": "P2",
          "claim": "the consumer's CONTROL surface is the partition {0} | {non-zero} — the boolean, not the exact integer",
          "assertions": [
            {
              "path": "returns",
              "op": "equals",
              "value": [
                "$ok"
              ]
            },
            {
              "path": "ok_predicate.binary_node_count",
              "op": "equals",
              "value": 1
            },
            {
              "path": "ok_predicate.left",
              "op": "equals",
              "value": "$code"
            },
            {
              "path": "ok_predicate.operator",
              "op": "equals",
              "value": "Ieq"
            },
            {
              "path": "ok_predicate.right",
              "op": "equals",
              "value": "0"
            }
          ],
          "_why_the_predicate_and_not_just_the_return": "returning a boolean is the CODOMAIN; the partition is the predicate that produces it. A mutation may preserve the codomain exactly and still move the cut — `$code -le 2` still returns a boolean and still returns `$ok`, but the observer is then a different observer, and observer.success_failure as recorded here is false of it. This is the property the fail-before test bites."
        },
        {
          "id": "P3",
          "claim": "the exact integer survives, but only to a non-control surface (console/log)",
          "assertions": [
            {
              "path": "host_writes_carrying_code",
              "op": "at_least",
              "value": 1
            }
          ],
          "_note": "this is what makes the consumer occupy TWO observer classes at once. This specimen gives an entity only to the control surface; the log surface has none, because the audit found the observer representation cannot express one consumer in two classes. Left visible rather than flattened silently."
        },
        {
          "id": "P4",
          "claim": "the anchor has 6 call sites, of which exactly 4 pass the subject as argv[0]",
          "assertions": [
            {
              "path": "call_sites_total",
              "op": "equals",
              "value": 6
            },
            {
              "path": "call_site_first_argv",
              "op": "count_of_value",
              "value": "main.py",
              "expected": 4
            }
          ],
          "_correction_on_record": "the quantum record said 'five invocations' in its third edition and 'six invocations of main.py' in its fourth. Both FALSE: six Run-Step call sites, four of which pass main.py — the other two run pytest and scripts/audit_release.py. The number was wrong twice in prose and nothing could catch it; here it is derived from the AST and a wrong number is a BROKEN link."
        }
      ]
